# @corpus current
# `skip = (p) while b = :peek() and p(b) .pos += 1`. A loop is the one
# statement with no expression spelling, so a callable whose whole body is a
# loop can only be written this way.
#
# THE DEGENERATE READING IS NOT A PARSE ERROR — it is a program that compiles
# and runs. `token_can_start_func_body` had no `while`/`for`, so `(n: i64)`
# scanned as a GROUPING paren: `step = (n: i64)` bound the parameter name as a
# value and the loop became a SIBLING STATEMENT at file scope. `duo check`
# reported that clean; only the C backend failed, and only on a later line.
#
# Which is why the first number is the one that matters. A loop that ran as a
# file-scope statement has already finished before `main` starts, so it prints
#
#   3 3 …   the loop ran at declaration time — the degenerate reading
#   0 3 …   the loop ran when the callable was CALLED — the correct one
#
# and the two are indistinguishable from the final value alone. The third
# number does the same job for `for`: 2+3+4 = 9 requires every iteration, so a
# body that ran once prints 2 and one that never ran prints 0.
# expect: 0 3 9

tick = 0
step = (n: i64)
  while tick < n
    tick = tick + 1

grid = 0
xs = { 2, 3, 4 }
fill = (t)
  for i, v in t
    grid = grid + v

main: i64 = ()
  before = tick
  step(3)
  after = tick
  fill(xs)
  print("{before} {after} {grid}")
  0
